[Unit]
Description=Garden terminal-worktree sweeper (reclaim completed, doomed, and orphaned checkouts)

[Service]
Type=oneshot
SuccessExitStatus=143 130 SIGTERM SIGINT
Environment=GARDEN_ROOT=@GARDEN_ROOT@
#
# RUNS ON EVERY HOST — deliberately NOT leader-gated (like garden-sysop and
# garden-state-clone-keeper). The garbage this reclaims is LOCAL to every host:
# each host generates its own gardener-wt-<base> / project-wt-* checkouts in its
# OWN $GARDEN_SCRATCH and its own garden-root worktrees, and only that host can
# see or remove them. Nothing here is journal/fleet state, so there is nothing
# for a single leader to do on behalf of the fleet. A leader-only ExecCondition
# here starved every FOLLOWER of cleanup, so terminal residue accumulated without
# bound (endolin-garden2-5bcdff64: 100 project-wt-* dirs / 41 GB, never swept —
# the sweeper's `Result=exec-condition` on every tick). Crucially, the reaper is
# a leader-only singleton: when it dooms a job whose checkout lives on a follower,
# its cleanup_terminal_project_worktrees runs against the LEADER's filesystem and
# finds nothing, so a follower's doom/crash/cross-host-requeue residue can be
# reclaimed ONLY by the follower's own sweep. is-main-host was the sole thing
# preventing that.
# A sweep of a badly-leaked host (dozens of multi-GB checkouts to unlink) legitimately
# runs well past self-heal's 600s handler default, up to the 1800s TimeoutStartSec below
# — so disable that deadline here (see self-heal-run.sh header); TimeoutStartSec is this
# unit's bound.
Environment=SELF_HEAL_HANDLER_TIMEOUT=0
ExecStart=/bin/bash @GARDEN_ROOT@/scripts/jobs/self-heal-run.sh garden-worktree-sweeper -- @GARDEN_ROOT@/scripts/jobs/worktree-sweeper.sh
TimeoutStartSec=1800

